{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1585"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_546680139#0_964622256","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1585","rank":2,"snippet":": 1427.000000 , \"headings\": \"Religion in Public Schools Religion in Public Schools Prayer in School: Personal vs. School-Sponsored Prayers and Religious Expression by Student Speakers at School Events School Celebrations of Religious Holidays Religion in the Classroom and in Homework No Discrimination Against Religious Literature or Clubs Speaking With a Lawyer \" , \"segment\": \"Religion in Public Schools | Lawyers.com Research Education Law Religion in Public Schools<cover>Public schools may not promote religion, but they must allow students to practice their faith. Learn how the both sides of religious liberty affect prayers at school, students' expression of their faith, religious holiday displays, and more. By E.A. Gjelten, Legal </cover>Editor Updated: Feb 5th, 2019 Freedom of religion, guaranteed under the First Amendment of the U.S. Constitution, has two parts: Government may not either promote religion (the “establishment clause”) or prevent people from practicing their religion (the “free-exercise clause”). The U.S. Supreme Court has long recognized that the First Amendment applies to state and local governments—including public schools. But that hasn’t settled the debates about teaching and practicing religion in school. Controversies—whether about "}}
