{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1586"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_605128816#2_867791291","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1586","rank":4,"snippet":"57_605128816#2_867791291\" , \"end_char\": 2377.000000 , \"headings\": \"Can students pray in public schools? Can teachers say ‘Merry Christmas’? What’s allowed — and what’s forbidden. Answer Sheet Can students pray in public schools? Can teachers say ‘Merry Christmas’? What’s allowed — and what’s forbidden. \" , \"segment\": \"Support our journalism. Subscribe today. arrow-right There has been a great deal of misunderstanding about what is allowed and not allowed when it comes to <cover>religious expression in public schools ever since the U.S. Supreme Court banned school-sponsored prayer </cover>in public schools in a landmark 1962 decision, saying that it violated the First Amendment. In fact, in 1995, then-President Bill Clinton issued a memo titled “Religious Expression in Public Schools,” that said in part: It appears that some school officials, teachers and parents have assumed that religious expression of any type is either inappropriate, or forbidden altogether, in public schools. As our courts have reaffirmed, however, nothing in the First Amendment converts our public schools into religion-free zones, or requires all religious expression to be left behind at the "}}
