{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1435369545#4_2962267739","relevance":3,"reason":"This segment remains directly about religion in public schools and provides concrete evidence on how religion appears or is addressed there, including religious-holiday instruction, protection against religion-based harassment, and federal guidance on protected prayer and religious expression.","learned":"Schools may address religion through objective holiday instruction, religious-expression policies, and anti-harassment protections for students’ religious traditions, while celebrations of religious holidays are unconstitutional."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1589","reason":"The current query is producing authoritative and directly relevant school guidance, so continue to look for additional grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1589"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_1475310364#3_2374564885","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1589","rank":3,"snippet":"Can Do While <cover>public schools cannot mandate that students pray or read the Bible (or any other religious book), the law does allow individual students to read the Bible and pray on their own time. This is a right protected by the U.S. Constitution. The U.S. Department of Education lays out helpful guidelines on this in “Guidance on Constitutionally Protected Prayer in Public Elementary and Secondary Schools,” The guidelines explain in one part that “… teachers and other public school officials may not lead their classes in prayer, devotional readings from the Bible, or other religious activities. Nor may school officials attempt to persuade or compel students to participate in prayer or other religious activities. Such conduct is “attributable to the State” and thus violates the Establishment Clause.” The Education Department adds in its guidelines: “ Although the Constitution forbids public school officials from directing or favoring prayer, students do not ‘shed their constitutional rights to freedom of speech or expression at the schoolhouse gate…'” These laws protect not just Christian students, but also Jewish, Muslim, Buddhist, and children of all faiths. When Teachers Can Require Bible Reading So in practicing their right to religious expression, </cover>public school students “may "}}
