{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_546680139#3_964628949","relevance":3,"reason":"The Lawyers.com document is dedicated to religion in public schools and directly explains how religion may appear or be limited through graduation invocations, football-game prayers, religious-holiday celebrations, and religious symbols such as nativity scenes or menorahs in displays.","learned":"Public schools must be careful with religious holidays and symbols: explicitly religious symbols may be unconstitutional unless part of a broader secular holiday display, and school-arranged student prayers at events can violate the Establishment Clause."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1597","reason":"The holiday-focused query is producing direct grade-3 guidance on religious holidays, symbols, prayer at school events, and student religious activities, so continue to collect more evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1597"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_255626711#5_471654665","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1597","rank":4,"snippet":"\"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_255626711#5_471654665\" , \"end_char\": 3280.000000 , \"headings\": \"Your Right to Religious Freedom Your Right to Religious Freedom WHAT IS RELIGIOUS FREEDOM EXACTLY? HOW DO YOU KNOW THE GOVERNMENT IS \\\"ESTABLISHING RELIGION\\\"? IS IT CONSTITUTIONAL TO TEACH RELIGION IN PUBLIC SCHOOLS? CAN MY TEACHER START THE DAY OR A MEETING WITH PRAYER? CAN MY SCHOOL HAVE PRAYERS AT GRADUATION? WHAT IF WE PUT IT TO A VOTE? IS IT EVER OK TO PRAY IN SCHOOL? IS IT OK TO <cover>CELEBRATE RELIGIOUS HOLIDAYS IN PUBLIC SCHOOLS?</cover>WHAT ABOUT RELIGIOUS CLUBS OR BIBLE DISTRIBUTION? \" , \"segment\": \"IS IT CONSTITUTIONAL TO TEACH RELIGION IN PUBLIC SCHOOLS? No. The public schools are run by the government. Therefore, they must obey the First Amendment. This means that while they can teach about the influences of religion in history, literature, and philosophy -- they can't promote religious beliefs or practices as part of the curriculum. Since private and parochial schools aren't run by the government, the First Amendment doesn't apply to them. Also, students can be excused from some school activities "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"29/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_255626711#5_471654665","relevance":3,"reason":"The ACLU religious-freedom document is directly about religion in public schools and explains how religion may appear in curriculum: schools may teach about religion's influence in history, literature, and philosophy, but may not promote religious beliefs or practices; it also notes possible student exemptions from activities.","learned":"Public schools can teach about religion’s influence in history, literature, and philosophy, but cannot promote religious beliefs or practices as part of the curriculum."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1597","reason":"The holiday-focused query continues to return strong direct guidance on several forms of religion in public schools, so continue to collect additional grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1597"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_546680139#4_964631460","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1597","rank":5,"snippet":"With a Lawyer \" , \"segment\": \"However, some courts have held that school officials may prevent students from delivering invocations as part of graduation ceremonies or giving commencement speeches that included Christian comments meant to convert their classmates. And the Supreme Court has held that a high school violated the establishment clause by arranging for students to give invocations over the public address system at football games, even though students created and led the prayers ( Santa Fe Independent School Dist. v. Doe, 530 U.S. 290 (2000)). School Celebrations of <cover>Religious Holidays Public schools need to be careful when they celebrate </cover>religious holidays. Schools may not put up explicitly religious symbols (like a nativity scene or menorah) unless they’re part of a larger holiday display that’s not religious. A display that includes symbols from several different religions may be seen as having the secular purpose of promoting tolerance and respect for all religions. As for holiday programs, the rule is similar. In general, holiday assemblies may include religious music, literature, or art—as long as it has an educational purpose, such as furthering students’ knowledge and appreciation of diverse religious heritages. This standard can "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"30/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_546680139#4_964631460","relevance":3,"reason":"The Lawyers.com segment is directly about religion in public schools and gives concrete evidence on religious-holiday celebrations, symbols, and programs, including nativity scenes, menorahs, multi-faith displays, and holiday assemblies with religious music, literature, or art for educational purposes.","learned":"Religious holidays can appear in public schools through neutral educational displays and holiday programs that include religious symbols, music, literature, or art, but schools may not sponsor prayers or devotional religious displays."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1597","reason":"The holiday-focused query has produced multiple grade-3 results with concrete examples of religion in public schools, so continue until the list stops being productive."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1597"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_255626711#4_471653160","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1597","rank":6,"snippet":"\"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_255626711#4_471653160\" , \"end_char\": 2890.000000 , \"headings\": \"Your Right to Religious Freedom Your Right to Religious Freedom WHAT IS RELIGIOUS FREEDOM EXACTLY? HOW DO YOU KNOW THE GOVERNMENT IS \\\"ESTABLISHING RELIGION\\\"? IS IT CONSTITUTIONAL TO TEACH RELIGION IN PUBLIC SCHOOLS? CAN MY TEACHER START THE DAY OR A MEETING WITH PRAYER? CAN MY SCHOOL HAVE PRAYERS AT GRADUATION? WHAT IF WE PUT IT TO A VOTE? IS IT EVER OK TO PRAY IN SCHOOL? IS IT OK TO <cover>CELEBRATE RELIGIOUS HOLIDAYS IN PUBLIC SCHOOLS?</cover>WHAT ABOUT RELIGIOUS CLUBS OR BIBLE DISTRIBUTION? \" , \"segment\": \"In 1971, the Supreme Court decided Lemon v. Kurtzman which created three tests for determining whether a particular government act or policy unconstitutionally promotes religion. The Lemon test says that in order to be constitutional, a policy must: Have a non-religious purpose; Not end up promoting or favoring any set of religious beliefs; and Not overly involve the government with religion. IS IT CONSTITUTIONAL TO TEACH RELIGION IN PUBLIC SCHOOLS? No. The public schools are run by the government. Therefore, they must "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"31/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_255626711#4_471653160","relevance":3,"reason":"The ACLU religious-freedom document is directly about religion in public schools and explains the constitutional framework for when school policies promote religion, plus whether public schools may teach religion or teach about religion.","learned":"Public school policies involving religion must have a secular purpose, not favor religious beliefs, and not overly entangle government with religion; schools may address religion only within constitutional limits."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1597","reason":"The religious-holidays query is still producing directly relevant ACLU and legal-guidance segments on how religion appears in public schools, so continue while it remains productive."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1597"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_1639554712#6_3525005805","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1597","rank":7,"snippet":"graduation because of “the degree of school involvement.” Perhaps the most unusual case in the recent past involved a first grader who was not allowed to read a story from The Beginner’s Bible for an assignment in which students were asked to read their favorite stories aloud in class. Equally divided, the Third Circuit Court of Appeals sitting en banc, upheld the teacher’s exercise of judgment, rather than confronting the question of the place of religion in school. ( Hood v. Medford Board Of Education, 3rd Cir., 2000). <cover>Religious Holidays: Holiday observations in public schools have been a persistent bone of contention in many communities. Although schools may teach </cover>about the religious beliefs underlying religious holidays and may celebrate secular aspects of such holidays, schools may not observe holidays as religious events or promote such observance among their students. Religious Messages: Schools may not permanently display religious messages like the Ten Commandments.\" , \"start_char\": 3286.000000 , \"title\": \"The First Amendment in Schools: Resource Guide: Religious Expression in the Public Schools - National Coalition Against Censorship\" , \"url\": \"https://ncac.org/resource/the-first-"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"32/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_1639554712#6_3525005805","relevance":3,"reason":"The NCAC resource guide is dedicated to religious expression in public schools and directly explains how religion may appear or be limited there, including student Bible reading in assignments, religious holidays, secular versus religious holiday observances, and permanent religious messages such as the Ten Commandments.","learned":"Public schools may teach about beliefs underlying religious holidays and celebrate secular aspects, but may not observe holidays as religious events, promote religious observance, or permanently display religious messages like the Ten Commandments."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1597","reason":"The holiday-focused query is still producing grade-3 guidance documents with concrete examples of religious expression and limits in public schools, so continue to find more relevant results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1597"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_1639554712#7_3525007683","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1597","rank":8,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_1639554712#7_3525007683\" , \"end_char\": 5190.000000 , \"headings\": \"The First Amendment in Schools: Resource Guide: Religious Expression in the Public Schools The First Amendment in Schools: Resource Guide: Religious Expression in the Public Schools\" , \"segment\": \"<cover>Religious Holidays: Holiday observations in public schools have been a persistent bone of contention in many communities. Although schools may teach </cover>about the religious beliefs underlying religious holidays and may celebrate secular aspects of such holidays, schools may not observe holidays as religious events or promote such observance among their students. Religious Messages: Schools may not permanently display religious messages like the Ten Commandments. Stone v. Graham (1980). They may, however, display religious symbols in teaching about religion, as long as they are used as teaching aids on a temporary basis as part of an academic program. Teaching of evolution: In Epperson v. Arkansas (1968) the Supreme Court struck down a state law prohibiting teachers in public schools from teaching “the theory or doctrine that mankind ascended or descended from a lower order of animals,” on "}}
