{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1435369545#9_2962278413","relevance":3,"reason":"The OSPI guidance page is directly about religion in schools and explains how religion may appear in public school curriculum: it must be discussed neutrally, objectively, and factually, may arise in lessons on families, communities, cultures, and holidays, and may be integrated into social studies, history, literature, art, music, and comparative religion classes.","learned":"Religion may appear in public school curriculum when taught neutrally and academically, including in discussions of families, communities, cultures, holidays, social studies, history, literature, art, music, and comparative religions."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1598","reason":"The religious-accommodations query is still producing strong grade-3 guidance segments with concrete examples of how religion appears in public schools, so continue while the list remains productive."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1598"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1435369545#8_2962276124","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1598","rank":9,"snippet":"Schools Looking for information for school districts? Contact Information Religious Expression at School<cover>Religious Accommodations Harassment Based on Religion or Creed Resources & Support Frequently Asked Questions May teachers teach about religious holidays? Must public school officials make school facilities available during nonschool hours for religious use by religious organizations? Is it constitutional to teach about religion in public schools? What constitutes teaching about religion? How may the study of religion be integrated into the public school curriculum? What does the law not permit? What does the law permit? \" , \"segment\": \"Although the U.S. Supreme Court has consistently rejected efforts to teach religion in the public schools, it has permitted teaching about religion in the context of a public education. What constitutes teaching about religion? Teaching about religion must be clearly distinguished from teaching religion, which amounts to religious indoctrination and practice and is clearly prohibited in public schools. A program intended to teach religion, disguised as teaching about religion, will be found unconstitutional. Religion may be presented as part of a public educational program, with the goal of teaching students </cover>about the role of religion in the historical, cultural, economic and social development of "}}
