{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_1639554712#4_3525002382","relevance":3,"reason":"The NCAC resource guide is explicitly dedicated to religious expression in public schools and directly explains how religion may appear or be limited through school-sponsored prayer, Bible reading, moments of silence, student-led prayer at school events, and concerns about official endorsement.","learned":"School-sponsored prayer and devotional Bible reading are excluded from public schools; student-led prayer at school-sponsored events may also be unconstitutional if it appears officially endorsed, though some student-initiated prayer may be permissible."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1599","reason":"This resource-guide query produced a strong grade-3 result on religious expression in public schools, so continue for more direct evidence within the remaining judgment budget."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1599"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_1639554712#8_3525009378","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1599","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_1639554712#8_3525009378\" , \"end_char\": 5512.000000 , \"headings\": \"The First Amendment in Schools: Resource Guide: Religious Expression in the Public Schools The <cover>First Amendment in Schools: Resource Guide: Religious Expression in the Public Schools\" , \"segment\": \"Stone v. Graham (1980). They may, however, display religious symbols </cover>in teaching about religion, as long as they are used as teaching aids on a temporary basis as part of an academic program. Teaching of evolution: In Epperson v. Arkansas (1968) the Supreme Court struck down a state law prohibiting teachers in public schools from teaching “the theory or doctrine that mankind ascended or descended from a lower order of animals,” on the ground that it violated the Establishment Clause of the constitution. The decision was reaffirmed in Edwards v. Aguillard (1987), which required evolution to be taught alongside “creation science.” The Court rejected the state’s alleged secular purpose to protect academic freedom, finding it a “sham.” Religious Clothing and Symbols: Religious clothing and symbols, if not disruptive, are a protected form of expression. Even "}}
