{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_1639554712#9_3525010947","relevance":3,"reason":"The NCAC resource guide is explicitly dedicated to religious expression in public schools and directly explains how religion may appear through religious clothing and symbols, dress-code exceptions for religious articles, released time for off-campus religious instruction, and neutral access to public school facilities by religious groups.","learned":"Religion can show in public schools through protected student religious clothing and symbols, released time for off-campus religious instruction, and religious groups' use of school facilities when other community groups are allowed."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1599","reason":"The current resource-guide query continues to return grade-3 evidence on concrete forms of religious expression in public schools, so continue for one more likely relevant result within the remaining budget."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1599"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_1639554712#3_3525000638","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1599","rank":7,"snippet":"inhibit religion. This does not imply that the public schools may not teach about religion. Indeed, “the Bible may constitutionally be used in an appropriate study of history, civilization, ethics, comparative religion, and the like.” Stone v. Graham, 449 U.S. 39, 42 (1980) (per curiam). Schools may teach about religion, explain the tenets of various faiths, discuss the role of religion in history, literature, science and other endeavors, and the like, as long as it has a secular purpose to promote educational goals, and there is no effort to promote or inhibit any religious belief. School-Sponsored Prayer: Prayer and Bible-reading have long been excluded from the public schools. Engel v. Vitale (1962) and School Dist. V. Schempp (1963). Even a moment of silence for “meditation or voluntary prayer,” is impermissible if its purpose is to promote <cover>prayer.\" , \"start_char\": 1566.000000 , \"title\": \"The First Amendment in Schools: Resource Guide: Religious Expression in the Public Schools - </cover>National Coalition Against Censorship\" , \"url\": \"https://ncac.org/resource/the-first-amendment-in-schools-resource-guide-religious-expression-in-the-public-schools\" }"}}
